# RoomFlow STAGING environment template
#
# Copy this file to .env.staging and fill in every CHANGE_ME before deploying:
#   cp .env.staging.example .env.staging
#
# .env.staging is gitignored (see .gitignore) — this template is the only
# staging env file that is tracked. Never commit a filled-in copy.
#
# Consumed by: docker/docker-compose.staging.yml
# Run from the REPOSITORY ROOT:
#   docker compose --project-directory . --env-file .env.staging \
#     -f docker/docker-compose.staging.yml up -d
#
# Every value below is a placeholder. Do not weaken one into a real credential
# in this file.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# READ FIRST — SINGLE-QUOTE ANY VALUE THAT CONTAINS '$'
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Compose interpolates the values in this file a SECOND time (the file is read by
# --env-file, then ${...} inside the values is expanded again). That second pass
# applies to UNQUOTED and DOUBLE-QUOTED values; a SINGLE-quoted value is exempt,
# which is why single quotes are the form to reach for:
#
#   RIGHT: JWT_SECRET='$abc123'     -> taken literally: the container receives
#                                      exactly the characters written here
#   OK:    JWT_SECRET=$$abc123      -> also valid (Compose turns $$ into one
#                                      literal $), but this relies on the
#                                      double-interpolation semantics above, so
#                                      it is the version-dependent option
#   WRONG: JWT_SECRET=$abc123       -> expands the (unset) variable "abc123"
#                                      -> empty signing key
#   WRONG: JWT_SECRET="$abc123"     -> the same: double quotes do NOT protect a
#                                      value from expansion
#
# The compose file's ${VAR:?must be set ...} guards cannot reliably catch this:
# they test what this file supplies, not what that text expands to (whether the
# guard sees the raw or the expanded value depends on the Compose version).
# The worst cases:
#   * ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH  — see the callout on this variable below; a BCrypt
#                            hash is ALL dollar signs ($2a$10$...) and is the
#                            single most likely value to be mangled here.
#   * REDIS_PASSWORD       — expands to nothing => redis-server starts with NO
#                            authentication. The redis service re-checks the
#                            value it actually received and refuses to start, so
#                            this one fails loudly.
#   * JWT_SECRET           — an empty signing key kills the backend at startup.
#   * DB_PASSWORD / MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD / RABBITMQ_PASSWORD — the MySQL and
#                            RabbitMQ entrypoints refuse to initialise with an
#                            empty credential on a fresh volume.
# Rule of thumb: if a value contains '$', wrap the WHOLE value in single quotes.
# If you are unsure, do not put it in this file at all — export it from the shell
# before running compose, because an exported value is already interpolated and
# is never re-expanded:
#
#   export JWT_SECRET='...' ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH='...'
#   docker compose --project-directory . --env-file .env.staging \
#     -f docker/docker-compose.staging.yml up -d
#
# The same rule appears in the header of docker/docker-compose.staging.yml and in
# docker/README.md.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Image pinning — REQUIRED, no default
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The staging compose uses ${IMAGE_TAG:?...}, i.e. fail-fast: with this unset,
# `docker compose up` aborts before creating anything. Set it to the immutable
# 40-char git SHA that CI pushed (ghcr.io/niuyeyeplus/roomflow-{backend,frontend}
# are tagged with ${{ github.sha }}; `latest` also exists but must never be
# used for staging, because a mutable tag makes a deploy
# non-reproducible — you cannot tell afterwards which commit is running).
IMAGE_TAG=CHANGE_ME_git_sha

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Host port bindings (optional — every one has a working default)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# These defaults are chosen so the staging stack never collides with the dev
# stack on the same host (dev uses 13306 / 16379 / 15673 / 15672 / 8080 / 80).
# Middleware stays bound to 127.0.0.1; only the frontend is published publicly.
STAGING_DB_PORT=13307
STAGING_REDIS_PORT=16380
STAGING_RABBITMQ_PORT=15674
STAGING_RABBITMQ_MGMT_PORT=15675
STAGING_BACKEND_PORT=18080
STAGING_FRONTEND_PORT=8081

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# MySQL — consumed by the compose (DB_NAME/DB_USERNAME/DB_PASSWORD) and by the
# mysql container (MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD). Empty values are a hard failure: the
# mysql image refuses to initialise with an empty credential on a fresh volume,
# and the backend cannot connect at all. Both are guarded with ${VAR:?...} in the
# compose, so a MISSING value aborts the deploy before anything starts; what no
# guard catches is a value that is present here but expands to something else,
# which is why a generated password containing '$' should be single-quoted —
# DB_PASSWORD='p$ssw0rd' — see the top of this file.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# NOTE: DB_HOST/DB_PORT below are NOT read by docker-compose.staging.yml.
# Inside the staging network the backend always gets the service hostname
# `mysql` on port 3306 (hardcoded, exactly like the dev compose). These two
# entries are for host-side tooling — an SSH tunnel or a mysql client on the
# staging box — and changing them will not move the backend's datasource.
DB_HOST=127.0.0.1
DB_PORT=13307
DB_NAME=roomflow_staging
DB_USERNAME=roomflow
DB_PASSWORD=CHANGE_ME

# MySQL root password. MySQL 8.4 refuses to initialise with an empty root
# password and the compose healthcheck will fail until this is non-empty.
MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD=CHANGE_ME

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Redis
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# REDIS_HOST/REDIS_PORT are likewise host-side-only documentation; the backend
# receives `redis:6379` inside the staging network.
REDIS_HOST=127.0.0.1
REDIS_PORT=16380
# Must be non-empty: redis-server is started with --requirepass, and an empty
# password would mean a Redis with NO authentication that anything on the
# staging network could reach. This is the one variable with a runtime guard —
# the redis `command:` re-checks the value it actually received (after Compose
# interpolation) and refuses to start rather than come up open — so a mangled
# value here crash-loops loudly instead of silently. Single-quote a value
# containing '$': REDIS_PASSWORD='$ecret' — see the top of this file.
REDIS_PASSWORD=CHANGE_ME

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# RabbitMQ
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# RABBITMQ_HOST/RABBITMQ_PORT are host-side-only; the backend gets
# `rabbitmq:5672` inside the staging network.
RABBITMQ_HOST=127.0.0.1
RABBITMQ_PORT=15674
RABBITMQ_USERNAME=roomflow
# Guarded with ${VAR:?...}, so a MISSING value aborts the deploy; the rabbitmq
# image additionally refuses to initialise with an empty password on a fresh
# volume. Single-quote a value containing '$' — see the top of this file.
RABBITMQ_PASSWORD=CHANGE_ME
RABBITMQ_VHOST=roomflow

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# JWT signing key and token lifetimes
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# application-staging.yml resolves the key as
# ${ROOMFLOW_JWT_SECRET:${JWT_SECRET}} — ROOMFLOW_JWT_SECRET takes precedence
# when set. The staging compose passes JWT_SECRET only (the same variable name
# the dev compose passes), so there is a single source of truth.
# Generate a long random value, e.g.:  openssl rand -base64 48
# An empty value is a hard startup failure: the backend refuses a too-short HMAC
# signing key, the health check then fails, and the deploy is rolled back. The
# compose guards this with ${VAR:?...} (missing => the deploy aborts), so what
# remains to get wrong is a value that is present here but expands to something
# else: if the value you generate contains '$', single-quote it —
# JWT_SECRET='a$b' — see the top of this file. (base64 output never contains
# '$', so a base64 secret needs no quoting.)
JWT_SECRET=CHANGE_ME
JWT_ACCESS_TTL=15m
JWT_REFRESH_TTL=30d

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH — REQUIRED, hard startup failure if missing
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# application-staging.yml declares the Flyway placeholder
#   admin-password-hash: ${ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH}
# with NO default. Flyway resolves placeholders while running migrations at
# boot, so a missing value makes the backend FAIL DURING STARTUP — before
# /actuator/health ever answers — and the container restart-loops. The dev
# compose does not pass this variable at all, so copying that omission into a
# staging deploy is the single most likely way to break this stack.
#
# The value MUST be a BCrypt hash generated OFFLINE, never a plaintext
# password, and never a hash produced on the staging host from a secret that
# then gets logged. Generate it locally, e.g.:
#   htpasswd -bnBC 10 "" 'the-chosen-password' | tr -d ':\n' | sed 's/^\$2y/\$2a/'
#   # or: python -c "import bcrypt;print(bcrypt.hashpw(b'...', bcrypt.gensalt(10)).decode())"
# It is used to seed the initial admin account in the staging database only.
#
# ── ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH IS THE VALUE MOST LIKELY TO BE WRITTEN WRONGLY ───────
# Write the hash SINGLE-QUOTED so the container receives it verbatim — see
# "SINGLE-QUOTE ANY VALUE THAT CONTAINS '$'" at the top of this file:
#
#   ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH='$2a$10$N9qo8uLOickgx2ZMRZoMyeIjZAgcfl7p92ldGxad68LJZdL17lhWy'
#
# Escaping every '$' as '$$' also works, but it is the version-dependent option;
# exporting ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH from the shell before running compose is another
# safe alternative, because an exported value is never re-expanded.
#
# WHY THE CALL-OUT — a wrong value here is NOT stopped by the deploy's
# pre-flight check: a BCrypt-SHAPED value with an unescaped '$' is reported as a
# WARNING and the deploy PROCEEDS (see .github/scripts/staging-deploy-lib.sh),
# because this is the one value an operator cannot reasonably write any other
# way. The failure therefore surfaces later, and it is a slow, confusing one:
#
#   SYMPTOM  the deploy waits out the entire health-check budget, then fails and
#            the revision is rolled back.
#   CAUSE    the value the container received is not the value written above. If
#            it ended up empty, the backend dies while it is still starting, so
#            /actuator/health never answers; `restart: unless-stopped`
#            restart-loops the container, the frontend is held back by its
#            `depends_on: backend: condition: service_healthy`, and
#            docker/health-check.sh polls two URLs that cannot come up.
#            A hash that is mangled but still non-empty can instead let the
#            backend boot and seed the `admin` account with a hash that matches
#            no known password: the health check passes and nothing rolls back,
#            but the admin login is broken.
#   FIX      single-quote the hash here, re-deploy, then check `... logs
#            backend` to confirm the startup error is gone (or, in the second
#            case, log in as `admin` to confirm the hash took effect).
ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH=CHANGE_ME_bcrypt_hash

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CORS
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Required by the staging compose. Comma-separated list of origins allowed to
# make cross-origin calls to the API; an empty value means "no cross-origin
# requests", which makes browsers reject the SPA's API calls. Use the exact
# scheme+host+port the browser sees, e.g. http://staging.example.com:8081
CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=CHANGE_ME_https://staging.example.com
